{
  "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
  "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:00 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Gregory Silbert",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Comity-based abstention proceeds from\na simple premise that this Court has recognized\nsince 1885. When a complaint alleges that\nforeign parties harmed other foreign parties in\na foreign country, a federal court can decline\njurisdiction in favor of a foreign tribunal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Gregory Silbert",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, the FSIA's plain text tells us that it concerns only sovereign immunity from jurisdiction. It has no effect on non-jurisdictional prudential doctrines like comity-based abstention."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: So, if we do not think -- for example, if I don't think that comity existed pre-1976, do you think we have the authority to just create comit -- a doctrine such as that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Okay. Well, what could be other relevant facts and circumstances?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Silbert",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, let's say, for example, the controversy concerns a discrete piece of artwork and that piece of artwork is hanging in a gallery in Washington, D.C., and let's say if the -- if the possessor of that piece of artwork gives it back to the wrong party, that U.S. party could become liable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Silbert",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "plaintiffs would file a civil action, as they would in any normal case. Hungary has waived by constitutional amendment any statute of limitations to these claims. They would file claims under, we believe, the Hungarian Code of 1959, which was the first codified law in Hungary and which applies to causes of action that accrued before its enactment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But that's a little bit of a different question, right? That's the question about the preclusive effect and whether any preclusive doctrines would themselves bar the plaintiffs from seeking relief here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Silbert",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0007",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "if the Hungarian remedies turned out to be a sham or a fraud, then I think they could try to reopen these claims in the United States courts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For well over a century, this Court has recognized that when an American court encounters a case that raises serious foreign relations concerns, the Court may abstain from the exercise of jurisdiction as a matter of international comity if it determines that the case would be better heard in a foreign forum."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does that abstention predate FSIA?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If this doctrine is all about the effect on foreign relations, if I were a district judge and I received a motion asking me to abstain on comity grounds, my first question would be, what does the government of the United States think about the foreign relations impact of this -- of this lawsuit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "SNCF",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There's an amicus brief filed in this case by SNCF, the French National Railroad, and that amicus brief describes a case in the Seventh Circuit in which the district court appropriately dismissed claims that had been brought against SN -- SNCF."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What else in that circumstance should a court ask itself?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What could -- what could defeat that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. HARRINGTON: Right. We think Congress has already made these foreign policy determinations and has taken into account comity"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And as a fallback -- and it is an alternative argument because it would sort of undermine our argument about comprehensiveness and clarity of rules, but, as a backup argument, we're saying, you know, if you're going to dismiss a case based on foreign policy concerns, it really shouldn't be a court, with all due respect to courts, making that decision. It should be an elected branch of government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 74,
        "start": 74
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the courts in those cases gave dispositive weight to the United States' request that those suits be dismissed based on foreign policy concerns."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0003-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 80,
        "start": 80
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I have a question about the nature of this kind of comity doctrine especially as compared to forum non conveniens. So everybody agrees that forum non conveniens doctrine survives the enactment of the FSIA, and I'm wondering, why in your view? Is that because forum non conveniens doctrine is a background principle that's incorporated somehow into the statute itself, or is that because courts retain the power to develop it as a common law doctrine?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 83,
        "start": 83
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Constitution assigns authority over foreign policy to the elected branches. Here, Congress has decided that this type of claim -- this is a -- sorry, this is the type of claim that U.S. courts should hear. And the executive, even after being invited multiple times to disagree with respect to this specific case, has declined to do that. In these circumstances, a court should not be able to step in and disregard its statutory jurisdiction based on its own assessment of foreign policy concerns."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0004-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 84,
        "start": 84
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Here, Hungary took everything the plaintiffs owned, including possessions necessary to survive, such as shelter, clothing, and medicine, and the undisputed purpose of Hungary's takings was to bring about the physical destruction of Jews in Hungary. That is genocide."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0004-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 84,
        "start": 84
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the only U.S. interest that the Solicitor General's office or the Department of Justice has identified in this case in the 10 years of litigate -- litigating it is the moral imperative to provide victims of the Holocaust with some relief in their lifetime."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, I -- I did not hear my friend give you any real workable limiting principle or safety valve for her position, and I think the colloquies with Justice Breyer and -- and Justice Alito brought that out."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5eba10fd4534b9b4ea819a8bcee941e8f8f2da2ae7222dae45c491600fb9263e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5eba10fd4534-0004-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted. (Whereupon, at 11:21 a.m., the case was submitted.)"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2020-18-1447",
